<p>
  A handheld multimeter is built for portability first, measurement depth second. The Vartech V 981+ inverts that priority - a portable bench-top instrument that trades pocket-sized convenience for genuinely laboratory-grade capability, without being tethered to a bench.
</p>

<h2 id="what-30000-counts-buys">What 30,000 Counts on a 3.5" TFT Actually Buys You</h2>

<p>
  Most handheld multimeters display 2000 to 6000 counts of resolution. The V 981+ displays 30,000 counts on a 3.5-inch colour TFT dual display - roughly five to fifteen times the resolution of a standard field meter, resolving small changes in a reading that a lower-count meter simply rounds away. The dual display shows two measurement values simultaneously (voltage and frequency, for instance), removing the need to switch functions mid-measurement to see both numbers you actually need.
</p>

<Callout type="tip" title="When this resolution actually matters">
  Most field electrical work doesn't need 30,000-count resolution - a standard 6000-count True RMS meter is accurate enough for load testing, fault-finding and general diagnostics. The V 981+'s resolution earns its keep specifically in R&D, quality control and calibration-adjacent work, where a small drift or a tight component tolerance needs to be visible, not just "close enough."
</Callout>

<h2 id="frequency-and-data">300 MHz Frequency Measurement and PC Data Logging</h2>

<p>
  Frequency measurement to 300 MHz is far beyond what any handheld multimeter offers, putting RF signals, telecommunications frequencies and harmonic analysis within reach of a single instrument rather than requiring a separate frequency counter or spectrum analyser for a quick check. The built-in PC interface with data recording lets you log measurements directly to a computer - essential for production test documentation, calibration record-keeping, or simply reviewing a trend after the fact rather than writing down readings by hand as they scroll past.
</p>

<h2 id="portable-bench-instrument">Why "Portable Bench-Top" Is the Right Description</h2>

<p>
  The V 981+ runs from a built-in rechargeable battery with Type-C USB charging - the same charging convenience as a phone or laptop, from any USB power source, with no disposable batteries to keep in stock. That's what separates it from a true bench instrument that requires mains power and a fixed location: you get bench-grade resolution, dual display and PC connectivity, but you can still carry it to a test bench, a customer site, or a production line and run on battery power. Math functions and Run/Stop let you freeze a reading or perform on-screen calculations without a computer connected at all.
</p>

<h2 id="v981-vs-v980-vs-v92s">V 981+ vs V 980 vs V 92 S - Where Each One Fits</h2>

<p>
  Vartech's range gives you a clear resolution ladder below the V 981+. The V 980 offers 20,000 counts, ±0.1% DC voltage accuracy and a conductance (nS) function aimed at senior engineers and QC teams who need better-than-standard precision in a conventional handheld body - genuinely capable, just without the dual TFT display, 300 MHz frequency range or PC data logging. The V 92 S sits a tier below that: a 6000-count "Smart" multimeter with Intelligent mode that auto-detects the measurement function entirely on its own, aimed at fast repetitive field testing rather than precision work. Choosing between them is a question of how much resolution and connectivity your work actually demands, not simply "which is newest."
</p>

<h2 id="quick-reference">Quick Reference</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>R&D, calibration-adjacent work, production test documentation</strong>: V 981+ - 30,000 counts, 300 MHz frequency, PC data logging.</li>
  <li><strong>Better-than-standard precision in a conventional handheld body</strong>: V 980 - 20,000 counts, ±0.1% DC accuracy, conductance test.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast repetitive field testing, minimal setup</strong>: V 92 S - Intelligent auto-detect mode, dual display with bar graph.</li>
